{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user is executing a stitch task (#5173) from a code generation pipeline. The task requires modifying `cmd/seq/main.go` to implement edge cases and error handling for the `seq` utility, specifically:\n   - **srd019-seq R3.4**: Large sequences should produce output via streaming without buffering all numbers in memory\n   - **srd019-seq R4.1**: Non-numeric arguments must produce an error message to stderr identifying the invalid argument and exit code 1\n   - **srd019-seq R4.2**: Wrong number of positional arguments (zero or more than three) must produce a usage error to stderr and exit code 1\n   - **srd019-seq R4.3**: Exit code 0 on successful sequence generation; exit code 1 on any error\n   \n   Design decisions specify: errors go to stderr, error format matches GNU seq (`seq: invalid floating point argument: XYZ`), use `os.Exit(1)` not `log.Fatal`.\n\n   Acceptance criteria: AC1 (seq abc → error+exit 1), AC2 (seq with no args → usage error+exit 1), AC3 (seq 1 2 3 4 → usage error+exit 1), AC4 (seq 1 0 10 → error+exit 1), AC5 (success=0, errors=1), AC6 (go vet passes).\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils `seq` utility\n   - Differential testing against Homebrew GNU `gseq` reference binary via `pkg/testutils`\n   - SIGPIPE handling via `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - Streaming output via `bufio.NewWriter` for large sequences\n   - Error messages written to stderr matching GNU format\n   - Exit codes: 0 for success, 1 for all errors\n   - The execution constitution mandates: no git commands, 40-line function limit, SPDX copyright header, write all files before building\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **`cmd/seq/main.go`** (existing, 363 lines) — The