{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B9bNCbD35uxPwh8sXeYMYH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! GNU seq treats `-f` and `-w` together as an error: \"format string may not be specified when printing equal width strings\". But our SRD R3.4 says: \"When -f is given, -w is ignored (the format string controls the output width).\" \n\nLet me re-read R3.4 more carefully:\n> -f and -w are mutually exclusive in effect. When -f is given, -w is ignored (the format string controls the output width). When only -w is given, the default format is used with zero-padding.\n\nHmm, the SRD says \"-w is ignored\" when -f is given, but GNU actually errors out. \n\nSo there's a conflict here: the differential test (R4.3) is the ground truth since we need to match GNU byte-for-byte, but R3.4 explicitly says -w gets ignored rather than erroring. Since the differential testing is what matters for correctness, I should make the implementation error when both flags are given, even though that contradicts what R3.4 describes. match GNU behavior, I need to make -f and -w together an error condition rather than silently ignoring -w. Since R3.4 is still in_progress for this task, I should fix the implementation to align with GNU's stricter behavior and update the corresponding test expectations.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":676,"cache_read_input_tokens":41708,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":676},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"13f5bbc1-b2d8-4a91-bf97-10193c31abab","uuid":"fc7ba518-a662-4b1b-8a25-55ec26f88434"}
